Welcome to the wonderful world of Alpha/Linux - and the slightly less wonderful
world of RH5.0/Alpha.

> - I downloaded an Alpha/RH4.2 static binary (AOLServer 2.2.1,
> http://www.aolserver.com) which I thought would run on RH5 .... of course it
> did not (ldd tells me it is not a dynamic executable, but looking at the
> file showed me a /lib/ld-linux.so.1 ????) what is it ?? a x86 executable
> (when em86 was installed but not working, it kept trying to run it) ?

You need the ld-linux.so.1 from RH4.2 (its the old dynamic linker) and you
need the old libraries.

> work (same patchings as above). I then realized my clock was completely out
> ... I always get 20 years of difference between the ARC clock and the system
> clock. I downloaded the clock executable from gatekeeper.dec.com, but
> there's no difference ... I read it was due to a too old firmware ... I have
> the latest !! What can I do ? (/etc/sysconfig/clock ?)

AH - you fixed the wrong problem by downloading the new clock binary :-)
There is a flag which says that your using the ARC bios and it takes
20 years off.  (The new clock binary is needed if you get random changes
in your date when you boot up sometimes).

On the em86 front; I have 0.4 running at home, I hand hacked the 2.1.6x patches
which came with it into a 2.1.84 kernel - believe me it was painful, but it
worked.

