Hello,

> By the way, anybody having had trouble with disk druid while trying to
setup 
> an almost 2GB partition? Think I m gonna switch back to fdisk...
It took me about 20 tries to install Linux on an alphastation200.  I used
fdisk, diskdruid and diskdruid i386 (on RH 5.0).  My partition table is as
follows: /usr is about 1.6 GB, / is 80 or so and /var is the rest.
First I tried fdisk but I couldn't find the heads/cylinders/sectors info on
the hardrive so I looked up the constructor's website (IBM) but could find
nothing.
Next I used Diskdruid which autodetects everything, pretty cool I though
but the system seemed to hang while it was formating the /usr filesystem
(the disk wasn't even spinning !!)
So then I went to fdisk on an i386 with an SCSI adaptator (very rare on
student campuses...) and it said everything was badly aligned so I started
from scratch once again but using diskdruid (still on the i386).
It autodetected every thing although differently.
I switched the disk back to the alpha and checked it out with fdisk,
everything looked fine so I tried installing again and, again it seemed to
hang formating the /usr partition.  By then I was getting really fed up so
I took a book and started reading.  About 7 or 8 minutes later (or more,
can't really remember except that it was really long) the install program
came back to life and started downloading the packages through ftp.

To this day, I don't know if the way DiskDruid detected on the Alpha would
have worked if I had given it more time to format.  All I know is that the
alpha autodetect was more efficient than the i386 in terms of disk space
allocation.

So my recommendation is : if your problem is that it seems to hang while
formating, juts be patient and let it work.
Incidently, I believe someone else on the list had the same problem and had
to wait something like 15 or 20 minutes...

Philippe Girolami
