Philippe Girolami wrote:
> After trying various methods for booting an alphastation200 (which has both
> ARC
> and SRM) I have experienced very weird things...
> i) I load MILO by booting ewa0 and using tftp.  Everything is fine
> (perfect, actually).
> But when I give it a "shutdown -r now" it crashes when MILO has finished
> its
> countdown:
>         "Unable to handle the pagin request at virtual address..."
>         "reboot(335):  Oops 0
>         and then a dump of registers
> 

I had the same problem when I compiled milo with the "EMBEDDED" flag,
which packages the Linux kernel together with milo... altogether a
1MByte image.

There was a spot in milo where it assumes it needs to save .5MBytes of
memory... which in my case was an underestimate.

One way to solve a problem like this is to make sure milo has been
recompiled with all verbosity, plug in a null modem, and watch for any
messages across the serial line.

There are a lot of messages that get output before there's screen
output... the first is "floppy hack" (or something like that).  See if
any of these are relevant.

If none, and if you think this may be a milo memory allocation
problem, then go into the memory.c file and force it to not free any
memory upon initial kernel boot (except for the large cluster of
memory at the end).

If that solves the problem, then iteratively free clusters until the
bug appears, then figure out what was in the needed cluster.  If it's
a memory issue, as mine was, then this debugging strategy will help
find the problem.

Make sure to use the current version of milo, and recompile the
palcode.  If using the DEC video bios, recompile under RedHat 4.2,
otherwise 5.0 is OK with some obvious modifications.

Chris
