> On 26-Mar-98 Sheer Panic wrote:
> > I wouldn't call linux-axp the underdog.. we have converted all our news
> > servers to axp-linux because it performs so much better as a news server
> > than OSF/1. For running mail, we are looking at a linux-axp system with
> > OSF/1 compiled binaries, for the best of both worlds.
> 
> Wait... It performs better than your OSF/1 boxes?  On equivalent hardware and
> for an equivalent function?

Yes. OSF/1 doesn't perform as intellegent a disk buffering action, which
for a news feeder makes all the difference in the world. Perhaps if we had
a later rev of OSF/1 this wouldn't be an issue.. but I doubt it.

Not only that, but linux lets me tweak peices of the kernel, and linux MD
performs _much_ better than advfs striping. 

Unfortunately, OSF/1 compiles binaries that execute _much_ faster when
performing small tasks where the binaries must be repeatedly loaded, like
mail(pop3 daemon). I do not know why this is.

S.


