From: "Harvey J. Stein" <hjstein@bfr.co.il>
To: randy@carrera.com, lloyd@carrera.com, darryl@carrera.com,
        martinob@bloomberg.net, axp-list@redhat.com, bugs@redhat.com
Subject: RH5.0 - undefined refs behavior changed?


Under all versions of Linux, I've always been able to link a binary
with undefined references & it would run fine, assuming the program
never tries to execute the unresolved reference.

This was especially convenient when linking f2c'ed Fortran into a C
main, because libf2c.so contains a main which calls MAIN__, and f2c
creates a MAIN__ when compiling a Fortran PROGRAM.  Thus, MAIN__ is
unresolved & will never execute when main is defined in a C program.

However, this seems to have changed under RH5.0:

   hjstein@aldev5:~$ gcc -o foo foo.o misc_func.o -g -DUSE_F2C -DALPHA_LINUX -Xlinker -noinhibit-exec -lp2c -lopt -lf2c -lgdbm -lm
   /usr/lib/libf2c.so: undefined reference to `MAIN__'
   hjstein@aldev5:~$ ./foo <tst.in
   ./foo: error in loading shared libraries
   /usr/lib/libf2c.so.0: undefined symbol: MAIN__
   hjstein@aldev5:~$ 

Compare to the same under RH4.2:

   hjstein@aldev5:~$ gcc -o foo foo.o misc_func.o -g -DUSE_F2C -DALPHA_LINUX -Xlinker -noinhibit- exec -lp2c -lopt -lf2c -lgdbm -lm
   /usr/lib/libf2c.so: undefined reference to `MAIN__'
   hjstein@aldev5:~$ ./foo <tst.in
   <program goes on to process input and create useful output>

Is this the way it's supposed to be?  Any reason this was changed?
Any way to change it back?  It seems pretty dumb to have a
-noinhibit-exec switch for ld while simultaneously refusing to run
binaries containing undefined symbols.

Thanks,

-- 
Harvey J. Stein
Berger Financial Research
hjstein@bfr.co.il


