Sigh; I, too, have seen this before... :-\

> There are settings in the driver tulip.c to say use only 10base2 so
> I enabled these and recompiled but still get the same message but 
> with another message a few lines later saying 'using user specified
> media 10base2', still no working net. I don't know if this situation 
> is what these settings are for.

Actually, you can do it via command line options, such as:

	ether=0,0,n,eth0

where the "n" is one of the options from the "Media types" table on 
the author's (Donald Becker) website:

	http://cesdis.gsfc.nasa.gov/linux/drivers/tulip.html

There, you can pick up a rcent version of tulip.c; I recommend 083, as
the last one I looked at (087), has a problem that can crash the kernel,
on Alphas only, I believe.

You can play around with changing the media type option at boot time,
as I don't think you can hurt anything by choosing an invalid one.

> Does anyone know if this is a driver problem or is it something
> earlier? The booklet with the 10base2 adapter doesn't mention any
> jumpers on the motherboard and there is nothing to set in the
> AlphaBios (there are settings for connector type in SRM 
> - and if set to BNC the adapter does physically work from the SRM
> console) 

No, AFAIK, there are no jumpers on the mobo nor on the MII connector.

Another option might be to try the latest DE4X5 driver, say, from 2.1.91.
That one accepts no command line option, but may work better for you in
this instance, although you may have to port it back to the kernel you're
building, if its in the 2.0.xx series. Ask me for details if you decide
to go this route...

Good luck.

