Dear Bob

>>  21164PC Processor
>
>Is this a 21164A, or something different?
>What is it?

Here's a quick FAQ I always get asked about the SX vs. LX.

(q) What's the difference between the SX & LX?
(a)The main difference between the LX and the SX is the cache design. The SX
has 
a total of two caches, primary on chip cache consisting of 16KB instruction 
and 8KB data. There is a secondary 1mb cache on the motherboard.

The LX has a total of three caches, two build into the processor the third a 
2mb cache located on the motherboard.  The primary cache consists of 16KB 
instruction and 8KB data. The secondary cache is 96KB unified data and
instruction.

In addition the SX has a 533Mhz limit in processor speed.  The LX on the other
 hand will run up to a 600Mhz processor.

Performance is as follows:

PC164LX & SX
Processor     cache   	SPECint95  	SPECfp95
211PC-533MHz  1mb-7.5ns	12.4		16.1 <SX>
21164-533MHz  2mb-9ns	16.1		18.8 <LX>
21164-600MHz  2mb-9ns	17.9		19.9 <LX>


(q)Is the SX a true 64-bit processor?
(a)Yes.


(q)how many slots are on the SX & LX?
(a)both have two 32-bit PCI slots and two 64-bit PCI slots and two ISA.  Nothing
is shared.  In addition, all slots can run full length cards.

(q)Can I run 32-bit PCI cards in the 64-bit PCI slots?
(a) yes.

(q)what are the power requirements on both the SX and LX?
(a)The LX motherboard has a total power dissipation of 100W, excluding any
plug-in PCI and ISA devices.  The SX motherboard has a total power dissipation
of 90W, excluding any plug-in PCI and ISA devices.

Therefore, if you are looking to fill either an SX or LX a 300 watt p.s. is
required.

Check these sites for more specs and HCL for the Digital Motherboards:

http://www.digital.com/semiconductor/alpha/alpha-memlist.htm
http://www.digital.com/semiconductor/alpha/dsc-pc164sx.html
http://www.digital.com/semiconductor/alpha/dsc-pc164lx.html

